    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-matias-delgado"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-matias-delgado.jpg" alt="José Matías Delgado" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-matias-delgado">José Matías Delgado</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Painting_of_Jose_Matias_Delgado.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rafael Beltranena, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> grew out of the colonial province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, an intendancy of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> planters resented the merchant houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>. San Salvador saw an early independence rising in November 1811, associated with the priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-matias-delgado">José Matías Delgado</a>, and after independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1821 it resisted annexation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire">Mexican Empire</a>, its congress even voting in 1822 to seek union with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">the Federal Republic of Central America</a>, founded in 1823, El Salvador was the stronghold of the liberals, and San Salvador became the federal capital in 1834. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a>’s conservatives defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a> at Guatemala City in March 1840, the federation collapsed, and on 18 February 1841 a constituent assembly adopted a constitution declaring El Salvador a sovereign and independent state. The new state covered only about 21,000 square kilometres on the Pacific slope of the isthmus, with no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast, but it was densely populated by Central American standards. Surrounded by larger neighbours, it remained for decades entangled in wars to restore or prevent the union, and several of its leaders, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerardo-barrios">Gerardo Barrios</a>, saw themselves as heirs of Morazán’s federal project.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hacienda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hacienda.jpg" alt="Hacienda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">Hacienda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hacienda_Lealtad,_former_coffee_plantation_in_Lares,_Puerto_Rico_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Eloquent Peasant, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">Indigo</a> was El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>’s main export in the colonial period and for decades after independence, grown on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a> and by peasants and sold largely through Guatemalan merchants. From the 1850s, and especially under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerardo-barrios">Gerardo Barrios</a>, governments promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> on the fertile volcanic slopes, and by the 1880s coffee had overtaken indigo, whose market collapsed with the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synthetic-dye">synthetic dyes</a>. Laws of 1881 and 1882 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-zaldivar">Rafael Zaldívar</a> abolished the communal lands of indigenous villages and the <em>ejidos</em> of municipalities, opening them to private ownership. Historians debate how quickly land became concentrated, but by the early twentieth century a small elite, proverbially the ‘fourteen families’, controlled coffee processing, exports and banking, and coffee supplied the great majority of export earnings. Most Salvadorans were landless or land-poor labourers and smallholders, and population pressure drove large-scale migration to Honduras. After 1950 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and light industry diversified the economy, aided by the Central American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">Common Market</a> created in 1960. The civil war and later gang violence drove well over a million people to emigrate, mostly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and remittances now equal about a quarter of national income. El Salvador adopted the US dollar in 2001. It had about 6 million people at the 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, overwhelmingly mestizo and Catholic, with a large and growing evangelical minority.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chalchuapa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-chalchuapa.jpg" alt="Battle of Chalchuapa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chalchuapa">Battle of Chalchuapa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muertebarrios.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Revista Guatemala, 1887, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>’s army grew out of the federal wars, in which Salvadoran forces fought repeatedly against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Conflicts with Guatemala and Honduras continued after 1841: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> defeated a Salvadoran and Honduran army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-la-arada">the Battle of La Arada</a> in 1851, and in 1863 he besieged and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, overthrowing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerardo-barrios">Gerardo Barrios</a>. In 1885 Salvadoran forces repelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-rufino-barrios">Justo Rufino Barrios</a> of Guatemala, who was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chalchuapa">Battle of Chalchuapa</a> while trying to reunite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> by force. The army became a professional institution in the early twentieth century and, from 1931, the ruling force in the state. In January 1932 it crushed a peasant and indigenous uprising in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> districts; the ensuing massacre, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-matanza">La Matanza</a>, killed perhaps 10,000 people or more, though estimates vary widely. In July 1969 El Salvador invaded Honduras in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/football-war">Football War</a>, advancing quickly before an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organization-of-american-states">OAS</a> ceasefire. The civil war of 1980–1992 pitted a US-funded army, expanded to several times its prewar size, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farabundo-marti-national-liberation-front">Farabundo Martí National Liberation Front</a> (FMLN), formed in 1980 from five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> organisations. Elite units such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlacatl-battalion">the Atlacatl Battalion</a> committed massacres, notably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-mozote-massacre">El Mozote</a> in December 1981, and in November 1989 soldiers murdered six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> priests at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-american-university">Central American University</a> in San Salvador. The war ended in stalemate after killing about 75,000 people.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gerardo-barrios"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gerardo-barrios.jpg" alt="Gerardo Barrios" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gerardo-barrios">Gerardo Barrios</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gerardo_Barrios.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Italo Lopez Vallecillos, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For its first decades El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> was governed by short-lived presidents dependent on military chiefs and on Guatemalan approval. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerardo-barrios">Gerardo Barrios</a>, president from 1859 to 1863, was the first great liberal moderniser, promoting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, education and secular reforms until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Carrera</a>’s intervention overthrew him; he was executed in 1865. The conservative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-duenas">Francisco Dueñas</a> ruled until 1871, after which liberals held power permanently. The constitution of 1886, in force until 1939, gave the liberal coffee republic its framework, with presidents normally chosen by the outgoing government; from 1913 to 1927 members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melendez-quinonez-dynasty">Meléndez and Quiñónez families</a> held the presidency in succession. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pio-romero-bosque">Pío Romero Bosque</a> allowed a free election in 1931, won by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arturo-araujo">Arturo Araujo</a>, but in December 1931 a military coup brought the vice-president, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximiliano-hernandez-martinez">Maximiliano Hernández Martínez</a>, to power. Martínez ruled until a general strike forced him out in May 1944, and for nearly half a century the army governed through official parties, from 1961 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/party-of-national-conciliation">the Party of National Conciliation</a> (PCN), with elections usually managed. The open fraud of 1972 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-napoleon-duarte">José Napoleón Duarte</a> of the Christian Democrats closed the electoral road to reform. After the reformist coup of October 1979, civilian–military juntas and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-pablo-duarte">Duarte</a>, elected president in 1984, governed under strong US pressure. The constitution of 1983, much amended, remains the basic law.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/los-angeles.jpg" alt="Los Angeles" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hollywood_sign_(8485145044).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gnaphron, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-matanza">La Matanza</a> of 1932 became a defining event in Salvadoran memory: it led indigenous communities in the west to abandon visible markers of identity for generations and shaped both the anticommunism of the elite and the outlook of later revolutionaries, who named the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farabundo-marti-national-liberation-front">FMLN</a> after the communist leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farabundo-marti">Agustín Farabundo Martí</a>, executed in 1932. The civil war of 1980–1992 was a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> providing billions of dollars in aid to the government. The murder of Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscar-romero">Óscar Romero</a> while saying mass on 24 March 1980 made him a worldwide symbol of liberation theology and of the defence of the poor; he was canonised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-francis">Pope Francis</a> in 2018. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chapultepec-peace-accords">The Chapultepec Peace Accords</a> of 1992, negotiated under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> auspices, became a model for UN-brokered settlements, creating a new civilian police and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commission-on-the-truth-for-el-salvador">Truth Commission</a> whose report of 1993 attributed the great majority of abuses to the armed forces and allied death squads. Migration created a large diaspora in the United States, and the gang <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mara-salvatrucha">Mara Salvatrucha</a> (MS-13), formed by young Salvadorans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a>, spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> in the 1990s. Writers such as the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roque-dalton">Roque Dalton</a> gave Salvadoran experience a voice.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chapultepec-peace-accords"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chapultepec-peace-accords.jpg" alt="Chapultepec Peace Accords" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chapultepec-peace-accords">Chapultepec Peace Accords</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:XXIV_Conmemoraci%C3%B3n_de_los_Acuerdos_de_Paz._(24312587612).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Presidencia El Salvador from San Salvador, El Salvador, América Central, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The settlement of 1992 transformed El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> into a competitive democracy. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chapultepec-peace-accords">the Chapultepec Peace Accords</a>, signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> on 16 January 1992, the army was reduced and removed from internal security, the old security forces were abolished, a National Civil Police was created and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farabundo-marti-national-liberation-front">FMLN</a> became a legal party. The right-wing Nationalist Republican Alliance (ARENA), founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roberto-daubuisson">Roberto D’Aubuisson</a> in 1981, won every presidential election from 1989 to 2004, privatising banks and adopting the US dollar in 2001. The FMLN then governed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauricio-funes">Mauricio Funes</a> (2009–2014) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador-sanchez-ceren">Salvador Sánchez Cerén</a> (2014–2019). Both parties were discredited by corruption and by their failure to control the gangs, which made El Salvador one of the most violent countries in the world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayib-bukele">Nayib Bukele</a>, a former mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, won the presidency in 2019 outside the traditional parties. In 2021 his legislative majority replaced the judges of the constitutional chamber, which then allowed him to stand again despite the constitutional ban on consecutive terms, and he was re-elected in 2024. A state of exception declared in March 2022 led to the arrest of more than 80,000 suspected gang members and a dramatic fall in homicides, but also to mass detentions without due process. Bitcoin, made legal tender in 2021, lost its compulsory status in 2025, and in the same year the legislature removed limits on presidential re-election.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> shows how a small, densely populated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> republic produced some of the sharpest social conflict in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-matanza">Matanza</a> of 1932 to the civil war of 1980–1992, and then became a model first for UN-brokered peacemaking and later for hard-line security politics.</em></p>

